# PART 3 – The replication {#part-3-the-replication .darkslide background-color="rgba(0,0,0,0)"}

::: {.partlabel}
Part 3
:::

::: notes
Part three brings in the replication. The sceptical prior was an argument from inside the experiment; the replication is evidence from outside it, four times as many replies to new names in another year. Bayesian estimation has a natural way of using it, by letting the posterior of the original audit become the prior for the new data. We do that, then put both studies in one model, and then check whether that model can reproduce the data that it was fitted to.
:::

## The replication as new data {#the-replication-as-new-data .smaller}


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](housing-bayesian_files/figure-revealjs/d3-update-1.png){width=912}
:::
:::


::: {.small}
- **Sequential**: the posterior of the original becomes the prior of the replication, so that "each estimate becomes the prior for the next step" [@McElreath2020StatisticalRethinkingBayesian, p. 34]. Alone, the replication gives −0.1 points (−5.2 to +5.0); with the original as its prior, **−4.0** (−8.4 to +0.7)
- **Joint**: one model of both studies with a single gap gives −3.8 points (−8.2 to +0.7)
- Each estimate gets "a weight proportional to the inverse square of its standard error" [@GelmanEtAl2020RegressionOtherStories, p. 119], and the replication has about four times as many replies
:::

::: notes
The first route is sequential, and McElreath describes it as the way in which a Bayesian model learns: each estimate becomes the prior for the next step. The script carried the posterior of the original into a normal prior for the gap of the replication. On its own, the replication puts the gap at zero, give or take five points, the green curve. With the original as its prior, the gap is minus four, the burgundy curve. The second route puts both studies in one model with a single gap, and lands in the same place. Regression and Other Stories gives the arithmetic behind the agreement: in the simplest case the combined estimate is a weighted average, each estimate weighted by the inverse square of its standard error. The replication has about four times as many replies as the original, so it carries most of the weight. The burgundy curve, though, sits where neither study put its estimate, and the next slide asks why.
:::

## A gap for each study {#a-gap-for-each-study .smaller}


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](housing-bayesian_files/figure-revealjs/d3-ppc-1.png){width=912}
:::
:::


::: {.small}
- Replicated from the model with one gap, the white share of the original has a 90% interval from 45.1% to 60.6%, and the observed 61.1% lies outside it: **one gap cannot reproduce the original audit**. priorsense flags the updated model as well, with a "potential prior-data conflict"
- With a gap for each study, the replication gap exceeds the original gap by **19.1** points (95% credible interval 8.2 to 30.4): the two studies disagree, and a combined estimate of about four points describes neither of them
:::

::: notes
So check the assumption behind both routes, that the two audits measure one gap. A posterior predictive check asks whether the fitted model can reproduce the data that it was fitted to. On the left, the model with one gap: for each study and group of names, the bar is the ninety per cent interval of the share that the model replicates, and the point is the share that was observed. The white names of the original audit lie well outside their bar, and the Hispanic names just below theirs. A model with one gap cannot reproduce the original audit, and priorsense says the same in its own terms. The failed check names the next model, on the right, with a gap for each study, which reproduces all four shares. In that model the replication gap exceeds the original by nineteen points, so the studies disagree, and the combined estimate of four points describes neither of them.
:::

# PART 4 – Errors of sign and magnitude {#part-4-errors-of-sign-and-magnitude .darkslide background-color="rgba(0,0,0,0)"}

::: {.partlabel}
Part 4
:::

::: notes
The last part asks what a significant result from a study of the size of the original can say about the size of a gap. The two studies disagree, and their disagreement could have many sources: the names, the authorities, the six years between them. One of those sources can be assessed with the numbers of the case alone, and it is the subject of a short paper by Gelman and Carlin, which adds two kinds of error to the familiar idea of power.
:::

## Type S and type M errors {#type-s-and-type-m-errors .smaller}


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](housing-bayesian_files/figure-revealjs/d3-design-1.png){width=912}
:::
:::


::: {.small}
- **Type S error rate**: "the probability that the replicated estimate has the incorrect sign, if it is statistically significantly different from zero"
- **Exaggeration ratio**, or expected type M error: "the expectation of the absolute value of the estimate divided by the effect size, if statistically significantly different from zero"
- The true effect has to come from outside the study: "realistic external estimates of effect sizes" [@GelmanCarlin2014PowerCalculationsAssessing, pp. 642–643]
:::

::: notes
Gelman and Carlin propose reporting two errors beside the power of a design. The type S error rate is the probability that a significant estimate has the wrong sign. The exaggeration ratio is how much larger than the truth a significant estimate is on average. Both depend on the standard error of the study and on the true effect, and they insist that the true effect come from outside the study, not from the estimate that is being judged. The chart computes the three quantities for any true gap, at the standard error of the original comparison and at that of the replication, with the retrodesign package. Read the dashed line at four points, about where the combined estimate of part three sat. The original would detect a gap of that size about one time in eight, and when it did, the estimate would be about three times too large.
:::

## What a significant result says {#what-a-significant-result-says .smaller}

::: {.small}
- If the true gap were **4 points**, the original comparison would reach significance with a probability of 0.12. A significant estimate would carry the wrong sign with a probability of 0.026, and exaggerate the gap by a factor of 3.2 on average: a significant estimate of about 13 points
- The exaggeration ratio of the original is 6.1 at a true gap of 2 points and 1.02 at 20; that of the replication 1.7 at 4 points and 1.02 at 10
- "[P]roblems with the exaggeration ratio start to arise when power is less than 0.5, and problems with the Type S error rate start to arise when power is less than 0.1" (pp. 643–644): the original crosses the first line for any true gap below about 10 points
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
None of this shows that the original estimate is exaggerated: at a true gap of 4 points an estimate of 20 would be rare even among the significant ones. It shows that a significant result from a study of this size says little about the size of a gap, and between the two studies the names, the authorities and the year all changed.
:::

::: {.src}
@GelmanCarlin2014PowerCalculationsAssessing; the closed form of `retrodesign::retro_design_closed_form()`; companion/bayesian.qmd, section 6.
:::

::: notes
In numbers: if the true gap were four points, the original comparison would reach significance about one time in eight. A significant estimate would almost never have the wrong sign, but it would overstate the gap by a factor of three on average, so a significant estimate of about thirteen points is what this design produces when the truth is four. At two points the factor is six; only near twenty points does it approach one. The replication, with half the standard error, exaggerates little above ten points. Gelman and Carlin give a rule of thumb: problems with exaggeration begin when power falls below one half, and the original design is below one half for any true gap under about ten points. [advance] And a caution, before anyone overreads this: it does not show that the published gap is exaggerated. It shows that a significant result from a study of this size says little about how large a gap is.
:::

